{
  "id": "Compound::DB06292 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Dapagliflozin, a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor, is shown to bind to Cyp1a2 and Cyp1a1, which are involved in the metabolism of various substrates. These enzymes are part of the oxidoreductive and aromatase activities, both of which are linked to the participation of Cyp3a4. This suggests that Dapagliflozin may influence the metabolic pathways of substrates that are processed by Cyp3a4, potentially altering their bioavailability or efficacy. The evidence from the three paths indicates a consistent mechanism by which Dapagliflozin interacts with Cyp1a1 and Cyp1a2, which in turn are involved in the activity of Cyp3a4. The shared pathway from Cyp1a2 and Cyp1a1 to Cyp3a4 highlights a possible role for these enzymes in the metabolic processing of compounds that Dapagliflozin may affect. The integration of these pathways supports the notion that Dapagliflozin may modulate Cyp3a4 activity through the activation or inhibition of Cyp1a family members, which is a plausible mechanism for its effects on drug metabolism.",
